{"type": "sena", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": "The Good Old Days"}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "What's your Opinion?"}, {"type": "position", "content": 11}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 8}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.2"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Identify and describe events in a range of social and work contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": " Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations. "}, {"type": "secondary_2", "content": " Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations. "}, {"type": "secondary_3", "content": " Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations. "}, {"type": "secondary_4", "content": " Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations. "}, {"type": "secondary_5", "content": " Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations. "}, {"type": "secondary_6", "content": " Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations. "}, {"type": "secondary_7", "content": " Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations. "}, {"type": "secondary_8", "content": " Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations. "}], "type": "objectives"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": " Phrasal Verbs"}, {"type": "describer", "content": " Phrasal verbs consist of a verb and one or more prepositions or adverbs (called particles). They are common, especially in informal spoken English. The meaning of phrasal verbs is often completely different from the meaning of the verb alone. <br/>Phrasal verbs can either be separable or inseparable."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Separable phrasal verbs always require an object."}, {"type": "text", "content": "The object of a phrasal verb can go either between the verb and the preposition or after the preposition."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"underline\">Examples</span>:<br/><span class=\"italic\">I handed in my homework.<br/> I handed my homework in.</span><br/>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "If the object of the sentence is a pronoun, it must go between the two parts of the phrasal verb."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"underline\">Examples</span>:<br/><span class=\"italic\">I handed it in.</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Some phrasal verbs are inseparable, so the verb must be directly followed by its preposition or adverb. Otherwise, the meaning of the phrasal verb will be incorrect.<br/>Some inseparable phrasal verbs never use an object."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"underline\">Examples</span>:<br/><li>get up = get out of bed</li><span class=\"italic\">I get up at seven o'clock every morning.</span><br/><br/><li>take off = leave the ground</li><span class=\"italic\">The plane took off on time.</span><br/><br/><li>look out = beware! be careful!</li> <span class=\"italic\">Look out! That car is going to hit you!</span>"}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":6,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" <span class=\\\"strong\\\">  Phrasal Verb </span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"strong\\\"> Meaning </span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" to meet up\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" to meet someone\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"to act out\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" to recreate a scene or behavior\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"to come out\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" to be released\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" to sign up\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" to register\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" to show up\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" to attend\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" instruction_table_background intro_table_border\"}"}, {"type": "example", "content": "They would meet up with friends. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Can you choose which classes you sign up for?"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "The Present Perfect Tense vs The Simple Past"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use the Present Perfect tense to talk about an action or event that is connected to the present in some way.<br/><br/>You use the Simple Past tense to talk about a finished action or event <span class=\"strong\">not</span> connected to the present."}, {"type": "text", "content": "It is common to mix both the Simple Past and Present Perfect when you tell anecdotes or reminisce about the past."}, {"type": "text", "content": "When you can use the Present Perfect there is a connection between what happened in the past and now. You use time markers like <span class=\"italic\">for</span>, <span class=\"italic\">just</span>, <span class=\"italic\">ever</span>, <span class=\"italic\">during</span>, with the Present Perfect."}, {"type": "text", "content": "When you can use the Simple Past the time is always past. You use time markers connected with the past like:  <span class=\"italic\">then</span>, <span class=\"italic\">ago</span>, <span class=\"italic\">yesterday</span>, <span class=\"italic\">when I was \u2026 </span> with the Simple Past."}, {"type": "example", "content": "I've blogged about how things nowadays are different from the way they were in the past. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "I interviewed George, who talked about how he and his friends used to spend their days when they were young. "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "carefree "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "without worries "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We were carefree when we were children."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le08ob11aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words to review."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to sing along "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to sing at the same time as a piece of music "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We would listen to and sing along with the songs."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le08ob11aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to act out "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to recreate a scene or behavior "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Gordie was super at acting out Elvis songs."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le08ob11aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to meet up "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to meet with someone or some people"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We used to meet up at our secret hiding place."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le08ob11aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "innocent"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "pure; not corrupted "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Those were the days: young, innocent and free!"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le08ob11aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "dais"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a raised platform "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The teacher used to stand on dais at the front of the room."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le08ob11aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "blackboard"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a surface that teachers write on with chalk."}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": " Example: There was a blackboard at the front the classroom."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le08ob11aud07"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to sign up "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to register"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Students often signed up for whatever subjects they wanted."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le08ob11aud08"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "boundary"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a limit of a subject or area of activity "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Social media accounts give kids no boundaries within which to function."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le08ob11aud09"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "limit"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "boundary"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Being a teenager is about pushing boundaries and testing limits, right?"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le08ob11aud10"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "engenn7le08ob11re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents":